engine: decimal thousandths for rules lifted from 6.1 (Mudb0y 7760e07)

Ports the VM half of "Read the rest of 6.1's compiled shapes, and give
the machine thousandths": a fifth global-variable kind DG_FIXED (-5, a
whole number of thousandths in an eight-byte cell), the matching DK_FIXED
location kind, the delta_muldiv helper (a*b/c in 64 bits, rounded or
truncated), and the OP_MULDIV interpreter op that runs it. delta_new /
delta_delete / initGlobalVars learn the fixed cells, reusing the two
words IBM left unused (nfixed at 0x000c, fixed at 0x0024).

No behaviour change on its own: no rule in this tree names a fixed
variable yet, and OP_MULDIV is appended after OP_FTOL so every existing
op keeps its number. It is the foundation the lifted .up rules need.

diff --git a/src/delta/delta.h b/src/delta/delta.h
index 0000000..0000000 100644
--- a/src/delta/delta.h
+++ b/src/delta/delta.h
@@ -366,11 +366,39 @@ typedef struct {
 /* One global variable of the machine, as the language declared it. Every
    variable lives in the tail of delta_state as a cell: its type tag first,
    then its value, so that a pointer to the value always has the tag just
-   in front of it. There are four kinds and the tag says which. */
+   in front of it. There are five kinds and the tag says which. */
 #define DG_WORD      (-6)   /* a 32-bit value, in an eight-byte cell */
 #define DG_LONG      (-3)   /* likewise, but the language calls it a long */
 #define DG_SHORT     (-4)   /* a 16-bit value, in a four-byte cell */
 #define DG_COMPOUND  (-9)   /* a run of bytes, described separately */
+/* What ETI's 6.1 holds as a double, here a whole number of thousandths in an
+   eight-byte cell: no language IBM shipped declares one, and the signal path
+   is kept to whole numbers so that a machine with no floating point can run
+   every language the same. A thousandth holds every constant 6.1's rules
+   write but a few in set_global_constants exactly, which is why it is a
+   decimal scale rather than a binary one. */
+#define DG_FIXED     (-5)
+#define DELTA_FIXED_ONE 1000
+
+/* a * b / c with the product held in sixty-four bits, which is what a rule
+   lifted from 6.1 does its decimal arithmetic in: a product of thousandths
+   divided back by a thousand, a quotient multiplied up by one first. Rounded
+   to the nearest, half away from nought, where the original worked in
+   doubles; truncated towards nought where it truncated one to a whole
+   number. A division by nought answers nought, since there is no whole
+   number for the infinity the original would have held. */
+static inline int32_t delta_muldiv(int32_t a, int32_t b, int32_t c, int round)
+{
+    int64_t n = (int64_t)a * b;
+    int64_t an = n < 0 ? -n : n;
+    int64_t ad = c < 0 ? -(int64_t)c : c;
+    int64_t q;
+
+    if (ad == 0)
+        return 0;
+    q = round ? (an + ad / 2) / ad : an / ad;
+    return (int32_t)((n < 0) != (c < 0) ? -q : q);
+}
 
